WEBVTT

00:00:00.581 --> 00:00:04.212
An agent that never moves may avoid resets while learning almost nothing

00:00:04.582 --> 00:00:08.432
An agent that repeatedly crashes and gets restored may learn a rewarding

00:00:08.444 --> 00:00:12.630
policy while relying on extensive intervention A meaningful theoretical

00:00:12.641 --> 00:00:17.081
objective should rule out both shortcuts Nguyen and Cheng study performance

00:00:17.093 --> 00:00:21.371
regret and reset cost together asking whether both can grow sublinearly

00:00:21.452 --> 00:00:24.898
with the number of learning episodes Sublinear growth means that the

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:28.795
total divided by the number of episodes tends to zero A square-root

00:00:28.806 --> 00:00:32.865
curve is the familiar example it grows forever but more slowly than

00:00:32.876 --> 00:00:36.854
a straight line Its average rate falls toward zero This is already an

00:00:36.866 --> 00:00:40.254
important distinction from a promise of zero resets during training

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:44.347
or a finite last reset after which assistance is never needed again

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:48.567
The paper's contribution is a reduction It turns reset-free learning

00:00:48.579 --> 00:00:52.429
into a two-player online optimization problem If the players achieve

00:00:52.545 --> 00:00:56.453
suitable no-regret guarantees the learning system inherits bounds on

00:00:56.465 --> 00:01:01.252
both performance and resets We will derive the bridge identify the assumptions

00:01:01.264 --> 00:01:04.756
making it possible and use a small payoff example to understand the

00:01:04.767 --> 00:01:06.270
role of the reset penalty

00:01:08.219 --> 00:01:11.920
The theorem uses finite-horizon episodes but it does not assume that

00:01:11.931 --> 00:01:15.782
the environment is freely restored to a fixed start after every episode

00:01:16.163 --> 00:01:20.326
If no reset event occurs the physical state carries into the next round

00:01:20.499 --> 00:01:24.604
while the episode's time index restarts Consequently the initial state

00:01:24.616 --> 00:01:28.593
of each round depends on what the learner did before This adaptive sequence

00:01:28.709 --> 00:01:32.502
is central to the problem The environment has designated reset states

00:01:32.883 --> 00:01:37.011
Entering one ends the round early and invokes the reset mechanism The

00:01:37.023 --> 00:01:41.278
formulation counts at most one such event in an episode After intervention

00:01:41.544 --> 00:01:44.805
the next starting state can again depend on the interaction history

00:01:45.198 --> 00:01:48.621
Do not confuse these counted external resets with a learned backward

00:01:48.632 --> 00:01:52.968
controller physically moving through ordinary states This modeling choice

00:01:53.038 --> 00:01:57.223
is precise and useful but it is not the most general continuing average-reward

00:01:57.235 --> 00:02:01.883
process It is a finite-horizon framework embedded in ongoing physical

00:02:01.895 --> 00:02:06.220
interaction The assumptions and theorem should be read within that framework

00:02:06.601 --> 00:02:10.417
In particular the comparator and cost will be evaluated on the adaptive

00:02:10.428 --> 00:02:14.302
starts actually encountered rather than on a freely chosen fixed initial

00:02:14.314 --> 00:02:14.926
distribution

00:02:16.911 --> 00:02:20.542
For each encountered initial state compare the learner's expected task

00:02:20.554 --> 00:02:24.566
return with that of a fixed feasible Markovian comparator policy Sum

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:28.162
those differences across episodes then choose the best comparator in

00:02:28.174 --> 00:02:32.857
the set that avoids resets at all relevant starts This defines performance

00:02:32.868 --> 00:02:36.696
regret It is a comparison on the learner's encountered states not a

00:02:36.707 --> 00:02:40.176
counterfactual comparison between two completely different lifetime

00:02:40.188 --> 00:02:44.651
trajectories For reset cost assign a cost when the process reaches a

00:02:44.662 --> 00:02:49.299
reset state Because the episode stops at its first such event its cumulative

00:02:49.311 --> 00:02:53.982
cost is either zero or one The cost value is therefore the probability

00:02:53.994 --> 00:02:58.145
of needing a reset in that episode Summing those expected costs gives

00:02:58.156 --> 00:03:01.764
the paper's reset measure conditional on the relevant starting states

00:03:01.776 --> 00:03:05.777
and policies This expected quantity should not be silently replaced

00:03:05.788 --> 00:03:09.766
by an exact observed count on every random run The paper's learning

00:03:09.777 --> 00:03:13.547
guarantees have their stated probability conditions while the value-based

00:03:13.558 --> 00:03:17.571
reset measure already contains an expectation over episode dynamics

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:21.491
Keeping these layers separate prevents a high-probability learning statement

00:03:21.676 --> 00:03:25.526
from being mistaken for a deterministic assurance that no individual

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:27.191
run will experience a failure

00:03:29.149 --> 00:03:32.641
Suppose every action from a certain starting state inevitably causes

00:03:32.652 --> 00:03:37.000
a reset No algorithm can achieve a vanishing reset rate if that state

00:03:37.012 --> 00:03:41.382
appears every round The paper therefore assumes that a reset-free Markovian

00:03:41.394 --> 00:03:45.233
policy exists for the relevant sequence of episode starts This makes

00:03:45.245 --> 00:03:49.107
the comparison class nonempty and establishes that the requested behavior

00:03:49.118 --> 00:03:52.876
is possible in the modeled problem The assumption is stronger than saying

00:03:52.888 --> 00:03:57.097
the very first state has a safe action Unknown dynamics can lead the

00:03:57.108 --> 00:04:00.658
learner to later starts with different possibilities The theorem has

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:04.300
to handle those starts too Its feasibility assumption is attached to

00:04:04.312 --> 00:04:08.093
the sequence encountered under the reset mechanism not merely to the

00:04:08.105 --> 00:04:11.863
initial demonstration or a designer's favorite nominal configuration

00:04:12.256 --> 00:04:16.881
The formulation also treats bad absorbing states as reset states preventing

00:04:16.892 --> 00:04:21.044
a learner from hiding forever in a useless trap to report low intervention

00:04:21.055 --> 00:04:25.021
cost These details matter because they connect the mathematical performance

00:04:25.033 --> 00:04:29.045
measures to the intended practical question Without a sensible reset

00:04:29.057 --> 00:04:33.173
mechanism and meaningful encountered starts small regret could describe

00:04:33.185 --> 00:04:36.607
a comparison that says very little about useful autonomous learning

00:04:38.585 --> 00:04:42.724
For a fixed episode start write a constrained decision problem maximize

00:04:42.736 --> 00:04:47.685
task return subject to zero expected reset cost Since reset costs are

00:04:47.696 --> 00:04:51.986
nonnegative zero expected cost means no reset event under the feasible

00:04:51.998 --> 00:04:55.895
policy in that modeled episode The challenge is learning such behavior

00:04:55.906 --> 00:04:59.803
with unknown dynamics while the initial state changes adaptively from

00:04:59.814 --> 00:05:03.873
round to round Introduce a nonnegative Lagrange multiplier that subtracts

00:05:03.885 --> 00:05:08.475
a price times reset cost from reward For one fixed start a scalar price

00:05:08.487 --> 00:05:12.973
may suffice Across changing starts the paper uses a multiplier function

00:05:13.042 --> 00:05:16.465
of the initial state This is a substantive feature of the reduction

00:05:16.858 --> 00:05:20.477
Different starts can have different reward-versus-reset tradeoffs so

00:05:20.489 --> 00:05:24.525
one universal penalty is not the object used in the theorem The resulting

00:05:24.536 --> 00:05:28.340
Lagrangian is a payoff between a policy player and a penalty player

00:05:28.733 --> 00:05:32.757
The policy player tries to maximize reward after the reset charge The

00:05:32.769 --> 00:05:36.677
penalty player tries to minimize that same payoff Larger penalties make

00:05:36.689 --> 00:05:40.690
reset-prone policies less attractive We can understand this interaction

00:05:40.701 --> 00:05:43.997
with a two-choice example before dealing with the shared saddle point

00:05:44.008 --> 00:05:46.517
required across the sequence of decision problems

00:05:48.453 --> 00:05:52.477
Consider two abstract policy choices The safe choice earns expected

00:05:52.488 --> 00:05:57.044
reward point five and has zero reset cost The risky choice earns point

00:05:57.056 --> 00:06:01.924
five plus a bonus b and incurs a reset with probability p Its penalized

00:06:01.935 --> 00:06:06.514
payoff is point five plus b minus lambda times p These are deliberately

00:06:06.584 --> 00:06:10.191
simple policy-level numbers not a simulation of the paper's learning

00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:14.007
algorithm The risky choice wins when its bonus exceeds its expected

00:06:14.019 --> 00:06:18.783
reset charge Set the payoffs equal and solve the crossing penalty is

00:06:18.794 --> 00:06:23.119
b divided by p With bonus point three and reset probability point two

00:06:23.408 --> 00:06:27.467
the crossing is one point five Below that price the reward incentive

00:06:27.478 --> 00:06:31.583
dominates Above it the safe choice has the larger Lagrangian payoff

00:06:31.976 --> 00:06:35.468
This picture explains why a dual learner can influence exploration through

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:39.619
a changing cost price It also explains why an arbitrary fixed penalty

00:06:39.712 --> 00:06:43.366
can be inadequate If the price bound never reaches the needed range

00:06:43.562 --> 00:06:47.505
the risky option stays attractive In the full theorem the existence

00:06:47.598 --> 00:06:51.390
and representability of a suitable multiplier function are mathematical

00:06:51.402 --> 00:06:55.264
conditions not details that can be ignored after drawing the two lines

00:06:57.238 --> 00:07:00.730
For a single constrained problem saddle-point reasoning is familiar

00:07:01.135 --> 00:07:04.858
the policy seeks a high payoff and the penalty seeks a low one But a

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:08.269
sequence of different initial states produces a sequence of constrained

00:07:08.281 --> 00:07:12.964
problems In general their best policies and multipliers need not coincide

00:07:13.334 --> 00:07:17.404
A reduction to one online game would be much less useful without a common

00:07:17.415 --> 00:07:21.856
comparator The paper proves a shared saddle-point result for its particular

00:07:21.867 --> 00:07:26.203
reset-free formulation A Markovian policy and a multiplier function

00:07:26.215 --> 00:07:30.921
work across the feasible initial states The proof uses the special zero-reset

00:07:30.933 --> 00:07:34.795
constraint and the feasibility structure This common pair is what lets

00:07:34.806 --> 00:07:38.599
the later regret bounds compare all rounds against compatible objects

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:42.496
despite the adaptive initial-state sequence There is another subtlety

00:07:42.877 --> 00:07:46.774
A common shortcut in constrained optimization assumes a strictly feasible

00:07:46.786 --> 00:07:51.052
point Here reset cost is nonnegative and the constraint demands zero

00:07:51.411 --> 00:07:55.273
so a strictly negative cost is impossible The paper establishes the

00:07:55.284 --> 00:07:58.973
needed result without relying on that shortcut The existence of the

00:07:58.984 --> 00:08:03.286
shared saddle point is a theorem to justify not a generic fact about

00:08:03.413 --> 00:08:05.610
every constrained reinforcement-learning problem

00:08:07.556 --> 00:08:11.337
Primal regret asks how much more Lagrangian payoff a fixed comparator

00:08:11.348 --> 00:08:14.817
policy would have earned against the penalties actually played Dual

00:08:14.829 --> 00:08:18.575
regret asks how much less payoff a fixed comparator multiplier would

00:08:18.587 --> 00:08:22.310
have allowed against the policies actually played The signs differ because

00:08:22.322 --> 00:08:26.426
the policy player maximizes and the dual player minimizes Write the

00:08:26.438 --> 00:08:30.497
signs carefully before trying to use either bound Add the two expressions

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:34.336
The payoff of the actual policy paired with the actual multiplier appears

00:08:34.347 --> 00:08:38.568
once with a minus sign and once with a plus sign so it cancels What

00:08:38.579 --> 00:08:41.852
remains compares the comparator policy against played penalties with

00:08:41.863 --> 00:08:46.014
played policies against the comparator penalty This simple cancellation

00:08:46.095 --> 00:08:50.639
is the algebraic hinge of the reduction No regret means these accumulated

00:08:50.651 --> 00:08:54.490
disadvantages grow sublinearly for the required comparators It does

00:08:54.501 --> 00:08:58.491
not mean every individual round improves or that each player receives

00:08:58.502 --> 00:09:02.145
full information about an unknown environment The policy player still

00:09:02.156 --> 00:09:06.365
has to explore and learn an online sequence of decision problems That

00:09:06.377 --> 00:09:10.227
statistical work is essential when the reduction is instantiated as

00:09:10.239 --> 00:09:12.343
an actual reinforcement-learning algorithm

00:09:14.304 --> 00:09:17.843
For the performance bound choose the feasible shared comparator policy

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:21.913
and set the dual comparator to zero In the cancellation formula the

00:09:21.924 --> 00:09:26.503
comparator policy's reset cost vanishes The played policy's zero-penalty

00:09:26.515 --> 00:09:31.036
payoff is simply its reward What remains is precisely the reward comparison

00:09:31.048 --> 00:09:34.817
we wanted giving the performance-regret inequality displayed on screen

00:09:35.233 --> 00:09:39.570
For resets the paper uses a multiplier equal to the shared saddle multiplier

00:09:39.581 --> 00:09:43.605
plus one The original saddle inequality says the feasible policy is

00:09:43.617 --> 00:09:47.583
at least as good as a played policy after charging the original penalty

00:09:47.964 --> 00:09:52.069
Adding one extra unit of penalty leaves the played policy's reset cost

00:09:52.312 --> 00:09:56.197
as an additional nonnegative quantity in the comparator gap Summing

00:09:56.209 --> 00:09:59.909
that relation and applying the same regret cancellation bounds cumulative

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:04.522
expected resets by primal regret plus dual regret against the shifted

00:10:04.534 --> 00:10:09.275
multiplier This is the core reduction suitable no-regret learners control

00:10:09.344 --> 00:10:13.241
both objectives Notice that the two bounds use different dual comparators

00:10:13.623 --> 00:10:17.427
Dropping that detail would obscure why one inequality measures reward

00:10:17.681 --> 00:10:19.554
and the other measures intervention cost

00:10:21.488 --> 00:10:25.466
The abstract reduction still needs concrete players The paper instantiates

00:10:25.477 --> 00:10:30.079
them in a linear M D P setting using known features that represent transition

00:10:30.091 --> 00:10:34.843
dynamics rewards and reset costs through unknown parameters It also

00:10:34.855 --> 00:10:38.359
assumes a known feature representation and bounded convex parameter

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:42.186
set for a suitable optimal multiplier function These are structural

00:10:42.197 --> 00:10:45.990
assumptions about the problem class not properties of every neural robot

00:10:46.002 --> 00:10:50.373
learner The policy side uses optimistic value estimates to handle unknown

00:10:50.384 --> 00:10:54.570
dynamics and the dual side uses projected gradient updates Under the

00:10:54.581 --> 00:10:58.432
stated feasibility and linearity assumptions the theorem bounds both

00:10:58.443 --> 00:11:02.548
performance regret and expected reset cost by a rate proportional to

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:07.890
B plus one times the square root of d cubed H to the fourth and K up

00:11:07.902 --> 00:11:12.550
to logarithmic factors With dimension horizon and multiplier bound fixed

00:11:12.793 --> 00:11:16.794
this is square-root growth in the number of episodes Dividing by K gives

00:11:16.806 --> 00:11:20.910
a rate that decreases like one over square root K The constants and

00:11:20.922 --> 00:11:24.900
structural assumptions remain relevant This is not a theorem that arbitrary

00:11:24.911 --> 00:11:28.970
deep R L will eventually operate without intervention nor a claim that

00:11:28.981 --> 00:11:32.161
total resets stop growing after a finite training time

00:11:34.108 --> 00:11:38.513
In the theory laboratory set the risky bonus and reset probability then

00:11:38.525 --> 00:11:42.710
run the two-choice price game The policy uses an exact best response

00:11:42.722 --> 00:11:46.538
to the current price The dual update increases the price by learning

00:11:46.549 --> 00:11:50.758
rate times expected reset cost then projects onto a declared interval

00:11:51.151 --> 00:11:55.233
Because the payoff contains minus lambda times cost gradient descent

00:11:55.291 --> 00:11:59.361
on the dual payoff produces this plus sign The display plots the price

00:11:59.373 --> 00:12:04.056
trajectory and reports accumulated expected reset cost It does not sample

00:12:04.067 --> 00:12:08.704
crashes learn unknown dynamics or implement the paper's optimistic linear-M

00:12:08.716 --> 00:12:13.110
D P algorithm This deliberately small experiment isolates how a penalty

00:12:13.191 --> 00:12:16.891
can change the preferred policy Lower the price cap below the crossing

00:12:16.902 --> 00:12:20.972
value and observe why the risky choice can remain attractive indefinitely

00:12:21.354 --> 00:12:24.846
A separate curve shows square-root growth and its corresponding average

00:12:25.228 --> 00:12:29.321
Those lines illustrate asymptotic scaling only they are not numerical

00:12:29.333 --> 00:12:33.345
theorem bounds with hidden constants or measured benchmark results Watch

00:12:33.356 --> 00:12:37.114
the total continue rising while the average falls That one picture is

00:12:37.126 --> 00:12:41.254
the most useful antidote to interpreting sublinear resets as a guarantee

00:12:41.323 --> 00:12:43.786
that there will eventually be no more resets at all

00:12:45.738 --> 00:12:50.062
The theory lesson is a chain First define meaningful reward and reset

00:12:50.074 --> 00:12:54.664
costs on the encountered initial states Next assume a feasible reset-free

00:12:54.676 --> 00:12:58.469
comparator exists Then prove a common saddle-point structure across

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:03.198
those starts Finally use primal and dual regret bounds to control both

00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:08.020
performance and interventions A concrete linear-M D P algorithm supplies

00:13:08.031 --> 00:13:12.240
one way to satisfy the reduction's learning requirements Each link answers

00:13:12.252 --> 00:13:16.033
a different question Feasibility says the desired behavior is possible

00:13:16.426 --> 00:13:19.779
The saddle-point theorem says the sequence of constrained problems has

00:13:19.791 --> 00:13:23.942
compatible reference solutions The regret reduction translates online

00:13:23.954 --> 00:13:27.781
optimization performance into reinforcement-learning performance The

00:13:27.792 --> 00:13:31.631
linear assumptions make a particular statistical rate available Removing

00:13:31.643 --> 00:13:35.783
a link requires a new argument not simply reusing the final square-root

00:13:35.794 --> 00:13:39.506
expression Our final paper returns to a practical learning mechanism

00:13:39.853 --> 00:13:43.669
world models Instead of proving that a specific linear representation

00:13:43.888 --> 00:13:48.386
yields a reset bound More Free learns a predictive model and uses imagined

00:13:48.398 --> 00:13:52.248
trajectories to train goal-reaching behavior The challenge will be deciding

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:55.983
what to practice in imagination and where to collect real data while

00:13:55.995 --> 00:13:58.666
the physical world remains stubbornly unresettable
